ADDART({id:"tessellations",t:"Tessellations",c:"Mathematics",i:"🧩",w:"Tessellation",r:["shapes","mosaic","islamicart","bee","fractals"],
p:[["Shapes with no gaps","Tiles that cover a surface without overlapping."],["Only three regular shapes","Triangles, squares and hexagons tile on their own."],["Art and nature","From honeycombs to Islamic tiles and Escher prints."]],
l:"A tessellation, or tiling, is a pattern of shapes that fit together to cover a flat surface completely, with no gaps and no overlaps, and that could go on forever. Floors covered in square tiles, brick walls and honeycombs are everyday examples. Only three regular polygons, shapes with all sides and angles equal, can tessellate on their own: equilateral triangles, squares and regular hexagons, because their angles fit together exactly to make 360 degrees around each corner point. Combinations of regular shapes can form other patterns, called semi-regular tessellations, such as octagons with squares. Many irregular shapes tessellate too: every triangle and every four-sided shape can tile a plane. Tessellations have been used in art and architecture for thousands of years, from Roman mosaics to the intricate geometric tilework of Islamic architecture, such as the Alhambra in Spain. The Dutch artist M. C. Escher became famous for tessellations of interlocking birds, fish, lizards and other creatures. In nature, bees build hexagonal honeycombs, which use the least wax to store the most honey. Mathematicians still discover new tiling shapes; in 2023, a single shape nicknamed ‘the hat’ was found that tiles a plane only in a pattern that never repeats.",
s:[["Why hexagons?","Regular hexagons tile with three meeting at each corner (3 × 120° = 360°). Compared with triangles and squares, hexagons enclose the most area for a given perimeter, which is why honeybees’ hexagonal cells are so efficient."],["Islamic patterns","Islamic artists developed complex geometric tilings with stars and polygons, called girih and zellige patterns, in mosques and palaces from Spain to Central Asia. Some medieval patterns resemble mathematical tilings discovered only in the 20th century."],["Escher","M. C. Escher visited the Alhambra in 1922 and 1936 and was inspired by its tiles. He developed methods of altering shapes so they interlocked as animals, such as in his prints of birds turning into fish."],["Never repeating","In the 1970s, the British physicist Roger Penrose found sets of tiles that cover a plane without ever repeating the pattern. In 2023, amateur mathematician David Smith and colleagues found ‘the hat’, a single tile with this property."]],
k:[["Definition","Shapes covering a surface with no gaps or overlaps"],["Regular shapes that tile alone","Triangle, square, hexagon"],["Angles at each corner","Add up to 360°"],["Famous artist","M. C. Escher"],["In nature","Honeycombs"],["Recent discovery","‘The hat’ aperiodic tile (2023)"]],
d:[["1619","Johannes Kepler studies tilings"],["1936","Escher studies the Alhambra’s tiles"],["1974","Penrose tilings discovered"],["2023","‘The hat’ aperiodic monotile announced"]],
s2:[["Pentagons","Regular pentagons cannot tile, but 15 kinds of irregular convex pentagons can; the last was found in 2015, and in 2017 the list was proved complete."],["Soccer balls","A classic soccer ball covers a sphere with pentagons and hexagons, a related kind of tiling on a curved surface."]],
g:[["Tessellation","A tiling pattern without gaps or overlaps"],["Polygon","A flat shape with straight sides"],["Regular polygon","A polygon with equal sides and angles"],["Aperiodic","Never repeating"]],
f:["Squares, triangles and hexagons tessellate.","Honeycombs are hexagonal.","M. C. Escher made tessellation art."],
fun:"In 2023, a hobbyist discovered a single tile shape that covers a floor without ever repeating the pattern."});

ADDART({id:"fresco",t:"Fresco Painting",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🖌️",w:"Fresco",r:["renais","michel","mosaic","architecture","caveart"],
p:[["Painting on wet plaster","Colors become part of the wall."],["The Sistine Chapel","Michelangelo’s ceiling is the most famous fresco."],["Ancient to modern","From Minoan palaces to Mexican murals."]],
l:"Fresco is a technique of painting on wall or ceiling plaster. In true fresco, called buon fresco, the artist paints with pigments mixed with water onto freshly spread, wet lime plaster. As the plaster dries, a chemical reaction with carbon dioxide in the air binds the pigment into the wall, making the colors extremely long-lasting. The word fresco comes from the Italian for ‘fresh’. Because the plaster dries within hours, artists must plan carefully and work quickly, painting only an area that can be finished in a day, called a giornata, and joins between days can often still be seen. In ‘secco’ painting, by contrast, paint is applied to dry plaster with a binder, which is easier but less durable. Frescoes have been made for thousands of years, from the Minoan palace of Knossos in Crete and the houses of Pompeii to the Ajanta Caves in India. They reached a high point in Renaissance Italy, with works by Giotto, Masaccio, Raphael and Michelangelo, whose Sistine Chapel ceiling (1508–1512) is perhaps the most famous painting of all. In the 20th century, Mexican muralists such as Diego Rivera revived fresco for large public artworks.",
s:[["How it is done","Artists apply several layers of plaster, the last a smooth, fine layer called the intonaco. They transfer their design using a full-size drawing called a cartoon, sometimes pricking holes along its lines and dusting charcoal through them, then paint before the plaster dries."],["The Sistine Chapel","Michelangelo painted the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel in Vatican City between 1508 and 1512, standing on scaffolding, covering about 500 square meters with scenes from the Book of Genesis, including The Creation of Adam. He later painted The Last Judgment on the altar wall."],["Mexican murals","Diego Rivera, José Clemente Orozco and David Alfaro Siqueiros painted huge fresco murals in the 1920s and 1930s, telling Mexico’s history and the lives of working people in public buildings so everyone could see art."],["Conservation","Frescoes can be damaged by damp, salts, smoke and earthquakes. A major restoration of the Sistine Chapel from 1980 to 1994 removed centuries of grime, revealing bright colors that surprised many people."]],
k:[["Technique","Painting on wet lime plaster"],["Word means","Fresh (Italian)"],["Daily section","Giornata"],["Most famous","Sistine Chapel ceiling (1508–1512)"],["Famous artists","Giotto, Michelangelo, Raphael, Diego Rivera"],["Ancient examples","Knossos, Pompeii, Ajanta"]],
d:[["c. 1600 BCE","Minoan frescoes at Knossos"],["1305","Giotto completes the Scrovegni Chapel"],["1512","Michelangelo finishes the Sistine Chapel ceiling"],["1920s","Mexican mural movement begins"],["1994","Sistine Chapel restoration completed"]],
s2:[["The Last Supper","Leonardo da Vinci’s The Last Supper is not a true fresco; he painted it on dry plaster, which is one reason it began to flake soon after he finished."],["Lasting color","Some true frescoes have kept their colors for thousands of years."]],
g:[["Fresco","Painting on wet plaster"],["Pigment","A colored powder used to make paint"],["Mural","A large painting on a wall"],["Cartoon","A full-size drawing used to plan a painting"]],
f:["Fresco is painted on wet plaster.","Michelangelo painted the Sistine Chapel ceiling.","Diego Rivera painted fresco murals."],
fun:"Leonardo’s The Last Supper started flaking within his lifetime because he did not paint it as a true fresco."});

ADDART({id:"gospel",t:"Gospel Music",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎶",w:"Gospel music",r:["blues","jazz","rockpop","musicals","christ"],
p:[["Joyful religious music","Gospel celebrates Christian faith."],["African American roots","It grew from spirituals and church traditions."],["A huge influence","It shaped soul, R&B and rock and roll."]],
l:"Gospel music is a style of Christian religious music known for powerful vocals, rich harmonies, emotional intensity and joyful energy. It developed in African American churches in the United States in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, growing out of spirituals, the religious folk songs created by enslaved Africans, and hymns, combined with musical elements such as call and response, hand clapping and improvisation. In the 1930s, the Chicago musician and composer Thomas A. Dorsey, often called the father of gospel music, blended blues and jazz with sacred songs, writing classics such as ‘Take My Hand, Precious Lord’. Singers such as Mahalia Jackson, Sister Rosetta Tharpe and later James Cleveland and the Edwin Hawkins Singers brought gospel to audiences worldwide, and gospel choirs became famous. Gospel music played an important role in the American civil rights movement, its songs giving courage at marches and meetings. Its style deeply influenced soul, rhythm and blues, rock and roll and pop: many stars, including Aretha Franklin, Sam Cooke, Elvis Presley and Whitney Houston, started out singing gospel. Today gospel is performed around the world, with strong traditions in Africa, the Caribbean and Europe.",
s:[["Spirituals","Spirituals such as ‘Swing Low, Sweet Chariot’ and ‘Go Down, Moses’ were sung by enslaved people, expressing hope, faith and longing for freedom. The Fisk Jubilee Singers toured the United States and Europe from 1871, bringing spirituals to wide audiences."],["Mahalia Jackson","Mahalia Jackson, called the ‘Queen of Gospel’, sold millions of records and sang at the 1963 March on Washington, just before Martin Luther King Jr.’s ‘I Have a Dream’ speech; she reportedly urged him to ‘tell them about the dream’."],["Sister Rosetta Tharpe","Sister Rosetta Tharpe combined gospel singing with electric guitar in the 1930s and 1940s and is often called the ‘godmother of rock and roll’, influencing musicians such as Chuck Berry and Elvis Presley."],["Around the world","Gospel music is hugely popular in countries such as Nigeria, Ghana, South Africa and Jamaica, where it blends with local styles, and gospel choirs perform in many European countries."]],
k:[["Origins","African American churches, late 1800s–early 1900s"],["Roots","Spirituals, hymns, blues"],["Father of gospel","Thomas A. Dorsey"],["Queen of Gospel","Mahalia Jackson"],["Features","Powerful vocals, call and response, choirs"],["Influenced","Soul, R&B, rock and roll"]],
d:[["1871","Fisk Jubilee Singers begin touring"],["1932","Dorsey writes ‘Take My Hand, Precious Lord’"],["1947","Mahalia Jackson’s ‘Move On Up a Little Higher’"],["1963","Mahalia Jackson sings at the March on Washington"]],
s2:[["Call and response","In call and response, a leader sings a line and the choir or congregation answers, a tradition with roots in West African music."],["Grammy category","The Grammy Awards have had gospel categories since 1968."]],
g:[["Gospel","Christian music with roots in African American churches"],["Spiritual","A religious folk song of enslaved African Americans"],["Call and response","A leader sings and a group answers"],["Choir","A group of singers"]],
f:["Gospel music grew from spirituals.","Thomas A. Dorsey is called the father of gospel.","Gospel influenced soul and rock and roll."],
fun:"Mahalia Jackson is said to have urged Martin Luther King Jr. to ‘tell them about the dream’."});

ADDART({id:"countrymusic",t:"Country Music",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🤠",w:"Country music",r:["folkmusic","blues","rockpop","gospel","guitar"],
p:[["Songs of ordinary life","Stories of love, home, work and heartbreak."],["Nashville","The capital of country music."],["Fiddle, guitar and banjo","Its roots are in folk and blues."]],
l:"Country music is a popular music style that originated in the rural southern United States in the early 20th century. It grew from the folk songs and ballads of immigrants from the British Isles, the blues and spirituals of African Americans, and gospel hymns, played on instruments such as the fiddle, banjo, guitar and later the steel guitar. Country songs often tell stories about everyday life, love and heartbreak, family, home, work, faith, drinking and the open road. The commercial genre took off in the 1920s with radio and records: in 1927, the ‘Bristol Sessions’ in Tennessee recorded the Carter Family and Jimmie Rodgers, often seen as the beginning of modern country music. Nashville, Tennessee, became its center, home to the Grand Ole Opry radio show, broadcasting since 1925, and many record companies. Over the decades, country developed many styles, including bluegrass, honky-tonk, the polished ‘Nashville sound’, outlaw country and country pop. Famous artists include Hank Williams, Johnny Cash, Patsy Cline, Dolly Parton, Willie Nelson, Garth Brooks, Shania Twain and Taylor Swift. Country music is popular far beyond the United States, with strong followings in Canada, Australia, the United Kingdom and elsewhere.",
s:[["The Bristol Sessions","In 1927, record producer Ralph Peer set up a temporary studio in Bristol, on the Tennessee–Virginia border, and recorded local musicians. The sessions launched the careers of the Carter Family and Jimmie Rodgers, the ‘Father of Country Music’."],["The Grand Ole Opry","The Grand Ole Opry, a weekly radio concert in Nashville, began in 1925 and is the longest-running radio broadcast in US history. Being invited to join the Opry is a great honor for country musicians."],["Many styles","Bluegrass, created by Bill Monroe in the 1940s, features fast banjo and fiddle. Honky-tonk, heard in bars, has a raw sound. Outlaw country, led by Willie Nelson and Waylon Jennings in the 1970s, rebelled against polished Nashville production."],["Diverse roots","The banjo came from instruments brought by enslaved Africans, and Black musicians such as DeFord Bailey and Charley Pride were important figures in country music, a history increasingly recognized today."]],
k:[["Origin","Rural southern United States, early 1900s"],["Roots","British folk ballads, blues, gospel"],["Instruments","Fiddle, banjo, guitar, steel guitar"],["Capital","Nashville, Tennessee"],["Famous show","Grand Ole Opry (since 1925)"],["Styles","Bluegrass, honky-tonk, outlaw, country pop"]],
d:[["1925","Grand Ole Opry begins"],["1927","The Bristol Sessions"],["1940s","Bill Monroe creates bluegrass"],["1961","Country Music Hall of Fame established"]],
s2:[["Three chords and the truth","Country music is sometimes described as ‘three chords and the truth’, a phrase credited to songwriter Harlan Howard."],["Line dancing","Line dancing, in which rows of people dance the same steps together, is popular at country music events."]],
g:[["Country music","A popular music style from the rural southern United States"],["Ballad","A song that tells a story"],["Fiddle","A violin played in folk styles"],["Steel guitar","A guitar played with a sliding metal bar"]],
f:["Country music began in the southern United States.","Nashville is the center of country music.","The Grand Ole Opry began in 1925."],
fun:"The Grand Ole Opry has been broadcast every week since 1925."});
